<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="HYwP27vmh87ey8cztmiOZi" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="GxuaU6cVMxXA8kR9pAjl2P" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="FFd5cqLe0xyXZKH7Buy6CC" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <!--
    Three NTS nodes and no more. They are here to put an OBJECT on screen beside a field that
    has none - the whole argument of the chapter - not to teach geometry, which chapters 01-05
    already did. The document declares the library itself because a patch using NTS nodes needs
    its own dependency; measured in Tutorial 07, kept true here.
  -->
  <NugetDependency Id="QiMkwsC0JQuWi3w2kyLiDi" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Cg2quazPuKfbcKSPLaG0DG" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="JZbUlzVfD8TTM1cRp1MhSX">
    <Canvas Id="SiIWlW16bdxKzbwSlxjNuD" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="AkHWIWWYGWwfuXfYhMXVAY">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="LNpkcWxoCUJ7zLk4TZhkzb">
        <Canvas Id="MtoiRBFXvrOiG3GMFIGMiD" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="ILiOuGQ1lEIDQDgrbUYOMi" Bounds="-620,-60,540,1360" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="12 - What if space is not an object.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Every chapter so far handed you THINGS. A point, a path, a plot of ground, a runway, a&#xD;&#xA;quake - each one an object with edges, a name and a passport. This screen has none. There&#xD;&#xA;is nothing here to pick up. Move the mouse anywhere and a number comes back, because every&#xD;&#xA;place has a value, and the values are all there is.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE ONE KNOB IS RESOLUTION. Drag it down to 8. The landscape turns to blocks. Now move&#xD;&#xA;slowly inside ONE block: the number keeps sliding, and the block does not change at all.&#xD;&#xA;Drag Resolution up to 128 and the blocks shrink until the picture looks continuous again.&#xD;&#xA;Nothing about the field changed - only how often you asked it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;SAMPLING IS WHERE A FIELD BECOMES A RASTER. A raster is not a picture of the world. It is&#xD;&#xA;a field, asked a finite number of questions, told to remember the answers, and then asked&#xD;&#xA;to pretend that is everything. The gap you just watched - number sliding, block flat - is&#xD;&#xA;the part it threw away.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE CURSOR IS STILL AN OBJECT. The WKT box on the right is chapter 01, unchanged: one&#xD;&#xA;Point, with an identity you could save to a file and mail to a stranger. Nothing&#xD;&#xA;underneath it is an object at all. Two data models on one screen, and that is the chapter.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Set Resolution to 6 and read Samples: 36 questions for a whole world. Set it to 128 and&#xD;&#xA;  it is 16384. The field did not get bigger. Your bill did.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Watch Cell Size. It is 1.8 divided by Resolution, and it is the only thing deciding what&#xD;&#xA;  the picture can and cannot hold. Halve it and you quadruple the questions.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Take the WKT out to Tutorial 01, or to any patch that reads WKT. The point travels. The&#xD;&#xA;  field does not - there is nothing to write down except the whole grid.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  You asked one continuous function - here a noise function, on the earth an elevation, a&#xD;&#xA;  temperature, a rainfall - the same question at evenly spaced places, and drew the&#xD;&#xA;  answers. That is every raster ever made, the satellite image and the DEM included.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  This particular field was born digital. It has no units and no place: it is a shape, not&#xD;&#xA;  a measurement. No GeoTIFF reader exists in this family - said here rather than hidden.&#xD;&#xA;  Real elevation is closer than that sounds, though: a DEM also ships as ordinary map&#xD;&#xA;  tiles, and tiles this family can already read. That is a later prompt, after chapter 10.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  A VALUE MEANS NOTHING WITHOUT ITS METADATA. 0.42 is not a height, not a temperature, not&#xD;&#xA;  anything at all, until something OUTSIDE the grid says what it counts and where the grid&#xD;&#xA;  is standing. That something has four parts, and it has a name.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  field          a value at every place - no edges, no objects, no gaps&#xD;&#xA;  raster         a field sampled onto a regular grid and remembered&#xD;&#xA;  cell / pixel   one sample. It is an AREA, not a dot&#xD;&#xA;  resolution     how far apart the samples are&#xD;&#xA;  nodata         the cell that has no answer - which is NOT the cell whose answer is zero&#xD;&#xA;  DEM            a raster whose value is elevation&#xD;&#xA;  georeferencing grid + origin + cell size + coordinate system. A raster is a field that&#xD;&#xA;                 signed a contract about where it is - and that last word starts to bite&#xD;&#xA;                 in chapter 10&#xD;&#xA;  vector data    everything the earlier chapters made. The other half of the field&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  You have done raster GIS for years without the vocabulary: a texture, a height map, a&#xD;&#xA;  scalar field, a simulation grid, an SDF, a noise function sampled into a mesh. The only&#xD;&#xA;  thing GIS adds is the contract about where the grid is standing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.NetTopologySuite, for exactly three nodes - Coordinate,&#xD;&#xA;Point and Write WKT - and that is the point: the object model is a guest here. A unit&#xD;&#xA;needing one package normally belongs in that package's own help folder; the exemption is&#xD;&#xA;written into this pack's rules for the spine, and this chapter spends it to stand the two&#xD;&#xA;data models next to each other rather than to teach a node.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NO MAP IN THIS CHAPTER, on purpose. A basemap would put an object underneath the field and&#xD;&#xA;quietly undo the sentence the chapter is trying to say.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 10 (planned)  same place, different numbers - where the contract bites&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 05            a buffer is a field too, thresholded. Go and look again&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt A mountain      an elevation drawn as objects. Now you know what it is not">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
